faq:
  - q: "What are the dominant industries in the Denver and Colorado job market?"
    a: "Colorado's economy is diverse and growing rapidly. Key sectors: (1) Aerospace and Defense — Colorado is one of the most important defense markets in the US. Lockheed Martin (Space Division in Littleton), Raytheon, Northrop Grumman, Ball Aerospace, and the Air Force Space Command (Peterson and Schriever AFBs) create enormous demand for aerospace engineers, systems engineers, and cleared professionals. Colorado Springs is the center of the military and space command ecosystem. (2) Technology — Denver has emerged as a significant tech hub. Palantir relocated its headquarters from New York to Denver in 2020. Google, Salesforce, Ibotta, and hundreds of SaaS companies have Denver operations. Boulder is home to a dense startup ecosystem centered around the University of Colorado and NCAR. (3) Energy — Colorado has significant oil and gas production (DJ Basin, Wattenberg Field) alongside a growing clean energy sector. (4) Outdoor and Active Lifestyle Industry — Patagonia, VF Corporation (The North Face, Timberland), Recreational Equipment Inc. (REI), and dozens of smaller outdoor brands have Colorado offices or operations. (5) Healthcare — UCHealth, Denver Health, SCL Health, and Centura Health are major employers in the state's large healthcare sector."
  - q: "How is the Denver tech job market different from other tech hubs?"
    a: "Denver's tech market has several characteristics that distinguish it from SF, NYC, and Seattle: (1) Palantir's presence has elevated Denver's government tech and data analytics profile — Palantir's Denver headquarters draws talent and creates a cluster of government technology expertise that's unique to the market. (2) Boulder's startup ecosystem is dense for its size — the University of Colorado's entrepreneurship programs, NCAR, and the National Renewable Energy Laboratory (NREL) create a research-to-startup pipeline. Notable Boulder companies: Zayo Group, Cologix, and dozens of SaaS and climate tech startups. (3) Remote work is normalized — Colorado's lifestyle appeal has attracted remote workers from coastal tech companies, creating a Denver-based tech professional class that is often employed by non-Colorado companies. (4) Compensation is below coastal tech but purchasing power is improving — Denver tech roles pay 15–20% below SF equivalents, with meaningful cost-of-living offsets; however, Colorado's housing prices have increased substantially since 2020."
  - q: "What do defense and aerospace professionals need to know about Colorado's job market?"
    a: "Colorado is one of the premier defense and aerospace markets in the US, with unique characteristics: (1) Space is the dominant defense specialty — Space Force, NRO (National Reconnaissance Office), and satellite systems companies make Colorado the center of the US space economy. Space operations, satellite engineering, and space domain awareness roles are highly concentrated here. (2) Security clearances are essential for most defense roles — Colorado's defense community is almost entirely clearance-dependent. Active SECRET or TS/SCI clearances are significant competitive advantages. (3) Cleared talent commands premium compensation — professionals with active TS/SCI clearances in Colorado earn a meaningful premium above equivalent non-cleared roles. (4) Relationship-driven hiring is common — the Colorado defense community is tight-knit. AFCEA Rocky Mountain Chapter events, National Space Symposium, and AIAA conferences are important networking venues. (5) Non-traditional defense backgrounds are valued — defense contractors increasingly seek professionals with data science, machine learning, and commercial tech backgrounds to apply modern approaches to defense challenges."
  - q: "What is the outdoor industry job market in Colorado?"
    a: "Colorado's outdoor and active lifestyle industry is a unique employment sector that does not exist in most US markets: (1) Key companies with Colorado presence: VF Corporation (The North Face, Timberland, Vans — headquarters in Denver), Patagonia (distribution and some operations), REI (major distribution), Osprey Packs (Cortez), Smartwool (Steamboat Springs), and dozens of smaller brands. (2) Business roles in outdoor industry — marketing, supply chain, product management, finance, and operations roles at outdoor companies require a combination of business skills and genuine passion for the product and lifestyle. Candidates who don't authentically participate in outdoor activities are spotted in interviews. (3) Compensation is below comparable roles in tech or finance — outdoor industry business roles generally pay below equivalent tech or financial services roles, reflecting the premium placed on mission and lifestyle alignment. (4) Boulder and Denver are the primary clusters — most outdoor brand business functions cluster in Boulder and Denver, not in resort towns."
  - q: "What networking channels work best for Colorado job seekers?"
    a: "Colorado's professional networking has specific channels that are more effective than generic approaches: (1) The Denver-Boulder corridor — the I-25 and Highway 36 corridor between Denver and Boulder is where most of Colorado's professional community operates. Proximity to both cities is helpful for networking. (2) Colorado Technology Association (CTA) — the primary tech industry association with regular events. (3) AFCEA Rocky Mountain Chapter — the primary networking organization for defense and government technology professionals. (4) Startup Colorado — a network supporting Colorado's entrepreneurship ecosystem. (5) Natural Grocers / Outdoor Retailer events — the outdoor industry concentrates at trade shows (Outdoor Retailer in Denver) and through industry-specific associations. (6) University alumni networks — University of Colorado, Colorado State University, and Colorado School of Mines alumni networks are active and create strong professional communities across industries."
